// A type parameter is a BINDER, so `<Point>` legally declared a fresh `Point` shadowing the type —
// which is what Rust and C++ do too. The problem was the silence: inside the declaration `Point` meant
// the parameter, the call below bound it to `int32`, and the mistake surfaced as a clang error about
// `int32_t` not being a structure or union, pointing at generated C.
//
// This is also why a generic specialization cannot be spelled `fn f<Concrete>`: that form is already
// taken, and taken silently. Naming it here is what makes that readable.
type value Point { public int32 x; }

fn int32 area<Point>(Point p) { return p.x; }

fn int32 main() { return area(p: 5); }
